WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.410 --> 00:00:02.044
<v Examiner>Two marks.

2
00:00:02.044 --> 00:00:05.911
<v Examiner>Define an agonist and an antagonist, with an example of each.

3
00:00:06.361 --> 00:00:09.918
Pharmacodynamics is what the drug does to the body.

4
00:00:09.918 --> 00:00:13.614
An agonist binds to a receptor and causes a response.

5
00:00:13.614 --> 00:00:15.288
Salbutamol, or morphine.

6
00:00:15.288 --> 00:00:21.285
An antagonist binds to a receptor and causes no response: it sits there and blocks it.

7
00:00:21.285 --> 00:00:24.423
A beta blocker, like metoprolol, or naloxone.

8
00:00:24.423 --> 00:00:29.514
Joan's point on formative two: antagonist is the name of the whole group,

9
00:00:29.514 --> 00:00:31.885
which is why it's the best answer.

10
00:00:32.335 --> 00:00:32.936
<v Examiner>Two marks.

11
00:00:32.936 --> 00:00:36.964
<v Examiner>Define a side effect and an adverse drug reaction, with an example.

12
00:00:37.414 --> 00:00:42.062
A side effect is an unintended but predictable secondary effect,

13
00:00:42.062 --> 00:00:44.023
like a headache with G T N.

14
00:00:44.023 --> 00:00:48.816
An adverse drug reaction, or A D R, is an undesirable, unexpected,

15
00:00:48.816 --> 00:00:52.592
harmful response to a drug, and it must be reported.

16
00:00:52.592 --> 00:00:54.843
Like anaphylaxis to penicillin.

17
00:00:55.293 --> 00:00:58.651
The rest of the vocabulary, for the multi-choice.

18
00:00:58.651 --> 00:01:03.037
Potency: a highly potent drug needs a lower dose for its effect.

19
00:01:03.037 --> 00:01:08.724
Efficacy: maximal efficacy is the maximum response a drug can produce, its ceiling.

20
00:01:08.724 --> 00:01:13.795
A loading dose is a bigger first dose to reach therapeutic levels quickly.

21
00:01:13.795 --> 00:01:18.661
Therapeutic index: the gap between the helpful dose and the toxic dose.

22
00:01:18.661 --> 00:01:22.155
Tolerance: a decreasing response to repeated doses.

23
00:01:22.155 --> 00:01:25.239
Receptor down-regulation: with long-term use,

24
00:01:25.239 --> 00:01:28.940
cells reduce the number of receptors on their surface.

25
00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:33.942
A toxic effect: too much drug, like respiratory depression with morphine.

26
00:01:33.942 --> 00:01:39.081
An idiosyncratic reaction: an unpredictable, genetically abnormal response.

27
00:01:39.081 --> 00:01:41.480
Teratogenic: harms the unborn baby.

28
00:01:41.480 --> 00:01:46.208
Synergistic: two drugs together have a bigger effect than separately.

29
00:01:46.208 --> 00:01:51.759
And prophylaxis: prevention of a disease before it happens, like the flu vaccine.

30
00:01:52.209 --> 00:01:55.882
Pharmacodynamics, what the drug does to the body.

31
00:01:55.882 --> 00:01:59.929
Agonist: binds and causes a response, like salbutamol.

32
00:01:59.929 --> 00:02:04.577
Antagonist: binds and blocks, like a beta blocker or naloxone.

33
00:02:04.577 --> 00:02:07.575
Side effect: predictable and unintended.

34
00:02:07.575 --> 00:02:11.623
Adverse drug reaction: unexpected, harmful, report it.

35
00:02:11.623 --> 00:02:14.172
Potency: high potency, lower dose.

36
00:02:14.172 --> 00:02:16.496
Efficacy: the maximum response.

37
00:02:16.496 --> 00:02:18.819
Tolerance: decreasing response.

38
00:02:18.819 --> 00:02:21.743
Prophylaxis: prevention before disease.
